    "17786893180b60ea14": {
      "type": "_blocks",
      "blocks": {
        "ai_gen_block_c88e324_m8PFU4": {
          "type": "ai_gen_block_c88e324",
          "settings": {
            "container_width": 1100,
            "left_column_width": 45,
            "right_column_width": 55,
            "column_gap": 50,
            "desktop_width_percent": 100,
            "heading": "What this means when you are shopping",
            "heading_font": "serif",
            "heading_size": 42,
            "mobile_heading_size": 28,
            "heading_color": "#1A1816",
            "heading_indent": 0,
            "mobile_heading_indent": 0,
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/What_this_means_1.png",
            "image_max_width": 500,
            "heading_image_gap": 16,
            "mobile_heading_image_gap": 20,
            "body_text": "<p>If your fragrance keeps disappearing before lunch, you are almost certainly wearing an EDT on dry skin. The fix is to move to an EDP version of the same fragrance, to moisturise before you spray, or both. Twelve seconds of unscented lotion before application makes a bigger difference to longevity than most people expect.</p><p>If your fragrance feels overwhelming in warm weather or small spaces, you are probably wearing an EDP or Parfum concentration when an EDT would suit the moment better. The goal is not the most concentrated option. The goal is the right one for how and when you wear it.</p>",
            "body_font": "system_ui_n4",
            "body_size": 17,
            "mobile_body_size": 15,
            "body_color": "#2D2A26",
            "paragraph_spacing": 20,
            "mobile_paragraph_spacing": 15,
            "text_vertical_offset": 0,
